## 8628-8693 · Switched opencode+ninfer to OpenAI protocol to eliminate phantom empty message
I read `docs/research/tool-result-empty-message.md` at the user's request. The doc's core finding was an Anthropic-protocol `tool_use.id` collision — the API reuses the same ID across separate tool calls, breaking client-side `tool_use`/`tool_result` pairing, and the client's repair logic inserts a phantom empty user message. Status was unverified for ninfer.

I checked ninfer's tool_use ID generation in `Neroued/ninfer`. It uses `random_identifier("toolu_")` with a thread-local `mt19937_64` — each tool call gets a unique `toolu_&lt;64-bit random&gt;` ID. No collision possible. The suspected root cause (ID collision) was ruled out for ninfer.

I then traced opencode's message construction in `to-llm-message.ts` and `anthropic-messages.ts`. No pairing repair logic, no phantom message insertion. Tool results are correctly lowered to `{ role: "user", content: [{ type: "tool_result", tool_use_id, ... }] }`. One edge case: if an assistant message has only empty text (no tool calls), it's dropped entirely — but this only happens when there's nothing to send.

The conclusion: the phantom empty message is NOT in the model's context. The model is misinterpreting the Anthropic protocol's `tool_result` format (wrapped in `role: "user"`) as an actual user turn. This is a protocol-level design issue, not a bug in our stack.

U: it's worth to switch back into openai

I checked the OpenAI protocol's tool result format: it uses a dedicated `role: "tool"` — `{ role: "tool", tool_call_id, content }`. The model doesn't confuse it with a user message. OpenAI does NOT have this quirk.

I updated the research doc with the findings. The user then asked about the thinking-block leakage that originally caused the switch to Anthropic.

U: we run this template https://huggingface.co/froggeric/Qwen-Fixed-Chat-Templates

I checked the froggeric template and ninfer's response handling. Ninfer properly separates reasoning from content at the engine level via `OutputChannel::Reasoning` vs `OutputChannel::Content`. The template handles `reasoning_content` extraction for OpenAI format. Opencode properly parses `reasoning_content` vs `content` in deltas. The infrastructure is in place.

The conditions that caused the original leakage (default template + old ninfer) are gone. I switched the config: `@ai-sdk/anthropic` → `@ai-sdk/openai`, and fixed `variants.off` from `thinking: { type: "disabled" }` to `reasoningEffort: "none"`. The user tested with a write to `.tmp/test.txt` — no phantom message, no thinking leakage.

I updated the theme file (status → resolved, full mechanism chain) and the README index entry.

