## 8698-8824 · 2026-10-08 · OpenAI protocol switch reverted — missing thinking blocks traced to absent `reasoning`/`interleaved` config
The user reported thinking blocks disappeared in Paseo after the OpenAI protocol switch, flagging that their earlier confirmation of the switch was premature. I initially suspected a Paseo 0.10.x rendering gap for `reasoning_content` (OpenAI protocol) and started checking Paseo's changelog and provider source. The user clarified we're on opencode v1, not v2/master.

The user then directed a full revert to Anthropic. I edited `~/.config/opencode/opencode.jsonc` to revert the protocol. The user noted each step had produced a full cache bust, which I initially attributed to the OpenAI protocol breaking prefix cache alignment (thinking tokens generated but lost in the response → next turn's context doesn't match → full prefill). The user corrected the attribution: the cache bust was MC, not the protocol.

The user then pointed out I had never checked whether opencode was actually recording reasoning parts in the DB before concluding the protocol was broken. I checked the DB: 49,322 reasoning parts exist from Oct 8 (Anthropic era), but zero from Oct 9 (OpenAI era). The thinking was missing at the wire level, not just Paseo rendering. I then investigated what opencode's OpenAI provider needs for reasoning output. I initially looked at ninfer's source, but the user corrected the target: the issue is in opencode's OpenAI provider, not ninfer. In opencode's provider source I found two key lines: `reasoning: model.reasoning ?? false` (line 1342) — without `reasoning: true` in the model config, opencode doesn't request reasoning at all; and `interleaved` defaults to `false` for `@ai-sdk/openai` (only auto-sets `reasoning_content` for `@ai-sdk/openai-compatible` with "deepseek" in the ID), so opencode doesn't know which response field carries thinking. The user asked about media/video capabilities and emphasized verification before proposing action. I confirmed `attachment: true` and `modalities.input: ["text", "image"]` are already set in the config — media is configured, no additional flag needed. The two confirmed missing fields are `reasoning: true` and `interleaved: "reasoning_content"`. The user then asked to check ninfer's input capabilities for video support and directed me to use `gh` CLI (not raw API). The chunk ends mid-investigation of ninfer's input modalities via `gh`.

## 8825-8934 · 2026-10-08 · Ninfer provider switched to @ai-sdk/openai-compatible; native @ai-sdk/openai drops reasoning_content
The ninfer provider config was on Anthropic wire format. I confirmed from ninfer's source that it supports `image_url` and `video_url` input modalities on the OpenAI endpoint, so the OpenAI config needs `modalities.input: ["text", "image", "video"]`.

I presented a comparison table (npm package, reasoning, interleaved, variants.off, modalities.input) in-chat. The user asked where the doc was; I admitted it wasn't written to a file. They directed it to `ninfer-integration.md`. I added a "Protocol config differences (Anthropic vs OpenAI)" section between the config annotations and fork inventory, and updated the README index row.

Applied all 5 config changes to `~/.config/opencode/opencode.jsonc`: npm → `@ai-sdk/openai`, reasoning: true, interleaved: "reasoning_content", modalities.input → ["text","image","video"], variants.off → {reasoningEffort: "none"}.

After restart, thinking blocks were still missing. I ran a wire test directly against the ninfer OpenAI endpoint: `reasoning_content` arrives correctly in both non-streaming and streaming responses. The model and server are fine — the drop is in opencode 1.18.30's parsing. I traced through opencode's source: the `interleaved` capability is defined in `provider.ts` but the consumption in the stream parser is in `packages/llm/src/protocols/openai-chat.ts`. I was mid-investigation when the user said to revert.

Reverted to Anthropic config. The user then asked what OpenAI provider variant I had used, suspecting "completions or something like that." This was the key question: I had used `@ai-sdk/openai` (the native OpenAI API provider) instead of `@ai-sdk/openai-compatible` (for self-hosted OpenAI-compatible endpoints like ninfer). The `interleaved` auto-detection in opencode only kicks in for `openai-compatible` + "deepseek" in the model ID; for the native `@ai-sdk/openai` provider, the stream handling is different and `reasoning_content` is dropped.

Documented the provider variant finding in `ninfer-integration.md` (new section, updated table header to `openai-compatible`, added wire-path reference `openai-chat.ts:419-420`, marked OpenAI switch as tested-but-unresolved). Updated memory entry [1255].

Switched to `@ai-sdk/openai-compatible` with the same flags. After restart, thinking blocks appeared. Root cause confirmed: the provider variant, not the protocol.

Updated all docs to reflect the resolved state:
- `ninfer-integration.md` — provider variant section, OpenAI switch marked RESOLVED with root cause
- `tool-result-empty-message.md` — status updated to "Switched to OpenAI protocol, resolved"
- README index — both entries updated
- Memory [1255] — corrected to `@ai-sdk/openai-compatible` as the working config

Final state: OpenAI protocol (`@ai-sdk/openai-compatible`), thinking blocks working, tool results use `role: "tool"` (no phantom empty user messages), cache bust was MC not the protocol.

Follow-up: verified in ninfer source that both protocols normalize system prompts identically — OpenAI reads `role: "system"` from the messages array (`openai_chat_request.cpp:51`), Anthropic reads a separate `body.at("system")` parameter (`anthropic_messages_request.cpp:374-389`); both normalize to `ChatRole::System` internally.

## 8935-9006 · Opencode message roles: system fold, ignored flag, and &lt;system-update&gt; mechanism
Investigated how opencode handles mid-conversation "system" messages and the `ignored` part flag, driven by the user's interest in a persistent sliding-window mechanism.

Traced the source (v1.18.30): mid-conversation `system` messages are folded into the adjacent `user` message in both OpenAI (`openai-chat.ts:303-318`) and Anthropic (`anthropic-messages.ts:409-422`) protocols. The `ignored` TextPart flag (set via ACP `recordPartMetadata` at `acp/session.ts:159`) excludes a part from compaction (`compaction.ts:57`) and UI display (`message-v2.ts:206`) but does NOT exclude it from the LLM context — the `meaningful` filter in `to-llm-message.ts:96-99` only drops empty parts, not `ignored` ones. So `ignored` is not a non-triggering mechanism; the model still sees the text.

U: "no i'm interested in message which model should see, but not consider as a user message and not respond to it, e.g. it should be in context, but not triggering"

The clean "in-context, non-user, non-triggering" slot is the `system` role, but both protocols pin it to the top (OpenAI: first message only; Anthropic: top-level parameter). Mid-conversation system messages fold into `user` with a `&lt;system-update&gt;` XML wrapper (`shared.ts:120-121`): content is XML-escaped, text-only, and preserves temporal position. The comment calls it "visibly lower-authority user text" — a prompt-level convention, not a protocol-level role.

Wrote `docs/research/opencode-message-roles.md` + updated README index + saved memory (ID 1259). Key takeaways: `system` slot is the clean non-triggering primitive but top-pinned; `&lt;system-update&gt;` fold is the closest mid-conversation option but is prompt-level only; `ignored` does not help (model still sees text).

Then verified ninfer's handling: zero hits for `system-update`/`system_update` in the entire ninfer repo; the chat template (`chat_template.cpp:16-32`) renders by role only — `system`/`developer`/`user`/`assistant`/`tool`. A `&lt;system-update&gt;` block inside a user message is plain text in a user turn with no special handling. The engine passes it through as user text; the template renders it as a user turn, which primes the model to respond.

U: "maybe, or maybe not we're using swift finetune which specifically were trained on agentic dataset"

This changes the assessment: the Swift finetune's behavior toward `&lt;system-update&gt;` is a property of its training data, not default 27B behavior. If the agentic data included non-triggering context patterns, the model may handle them correctly. This is a live-test question — not resolvable from source inspection.

Started checking the published training data (tool calls at 9005-9006, arc ends here).

## 9007-9037 · Swift 1.5 training data confirms no non-triggering context pattern
Investigated whether the `&lt;system-update&gt;` fold is a viable non-triggering primitive for the Swift 1.5 finetune's sliding-window mechanism. Downloaded and inspected the training data (`ukisai/Qwen3.8-27B-multi-turn-agent-sft`, ~145 MB parquet, 15,209 agentic conversations). Found: zero `&lt;system-update&gt;` tags (7 string hits were all false positives — `systemd-system-update-generator` / `system-update-cleanup.service` file paths in terminal output), roles purely `user` + `assistant` (100,389 each, no `system` role), and no non-triggering context pattern (the 224 "for context" and 54 "ignoring this" hits were all incidental natural language in task descriptions).

Updated the research doc with the training-data finding and the conclusion that `&lt;system-update&gt;` fold is not viable; the viable path is a prompt-level instruction the model is tuned to follow. User clarified that "remove temporal data" meant delete the downloaded parquet file, not strip dates from the doc. Deleted `/tmp/agent-sft.parquet` (145 MB) and restored the fetch dates in the doc and README entry (the repo convention requires them). Saved a tooling preference memory (ID 1261): use `gh` for GitHub and `hf` CLI for Hugging Face, not raw `curl`.

