## 9080-9095 · 2026-10-08 · Jeff-Code (Pi fork): System 1 classifier architecture for Qwen 3.8-27B
The user shared a Reddit post about Jeff-Code — a fork of Pi (Mario Zechner) with "Jeff v1.3" adapters trained for Qwen 3.8-27B. The core idea: a fast 0.8B "System 1" classifier sits deep inside the agent loop, handling routing/decisions that would otherwise burn the slow model's time. Their numbers (1,242 paired tasks): quality flat (62.4% vs 62.8% pass rate, Δ −0.2, 95% CI −2.6…+2.1), speed 0.68× geometric mean (0.64–0.72 CI). Fetched the post via Paseo browser (Reddit bot-walls curl/webfetch). The user asked me to figure out how it's done and what model is used, as the decision criterion for whether it's worth looking at. Architecture: `jeff-base` v1.3 = fine-tune of Qwen3.5-0.8B (Apache-2.0), a single-forward-pass calibrated classifier (state + options in → probability per option, no generated text to parse). Two adapters per Qwen turn (~0.2s each, in `packages/coding-agent/src/core/jeff-first/`): (1) Step adapter (`jeff-adapter-code`): when confident, Jeff takes the next info step (read file, list dir, search, run tests/build) — writing/editing stays with Qwen; it chains steps so Qwen starts its turn with results already in front of it. (2) Thinking router (`jeff-adapter-code-router`): sets Qwen's thinking level per turn; full thinking only when "needs it" probability ≥ 0.6 → ~3/4 of turns run thinking-off. Trained by imitation (step labels = the step Qwen actually took next, built by code with no teacher model; thinking labels = the cheapest level "as good" as full). Plus loop-guard (6 steps back), runaway cut-off, and an 8000-token thinking limit that rescues the 32K output cap. Assessment: the thinking router is the high-value part for the user's setup (per-turn thinking-off at flat pass rate → less KV, faster decode, directly addresses the latency/KV bottleneck). The info-step offload cuts 27B prefills. Friction: it's a Pi fork (user runs opencode + ninfer), needs a separate Jeff server (one per GPU) + the two adapters loaded, the 0.68× is geometric-mean-dominated by SWE-bench (0.63×) with no speedup on Terminal-Bench 2.0 (0.96×) / SkillsBench (0.91×), and the 0.8B base is Qwen3.5 not 3.8 (adapters trained on Qwen 3.8-27B so should transfer, but base family differs). The reusable asset is the pattern (0.8B Qwen classifier in the loop, imitation-trained) + the published GGUFs (`mstrasser/jeff-base-gguf` + `jeff-adapter-&lt;name&gt;-gguf`), not the Pi codebase. The user then asked to document it as a notable usecase and raised a claim that PI made classifier integration in version 0.99 — I was starting to investigate the PI repo (`earendil-works/pi`) for this when the chunk ends.

