## 9130-9180 · 2026-10-08 · Blast-radius observer design: clean-context subagent as unbiased safety gate
User shared a Reddit link about classifiers for LocalLLM. I fetched the page via the Paseo browser (Reddit bot-walls curl/webfetch) and identified **Ekbasis** — a calibrated 27B "consequence oracle" that answers MCQ "what happens if I do X?" with calibrated confidence (ECE 0.04–0.07), escalating to the user below 0.5. 1,273 runs / 7 app domains: agent alone = 20–57% damage → +Ekbasis = 0–3%. I contrasted it with Jeff-Code (efficiency router, 0.8B, in-the-loop) vs Ekbasis (safety guardrail, 27B, tool-based).

I over-anchored on implementation-fit. The use case is the point: **consequence prediction as a safety guardrail** — a distinct axis from Jeff-Code's efficiency routing. Both are "System-1 beside the agent," different axes.

The key reframe: a "classifier" is just a stateless function `f(state, question) → judgment`. The main agent's context is polluted; a clean-context subagent sees only the slice you hand it — that *is* the unbiased observer. The one real gap is **calibration** (self-reported vs measured ECE), but the mechanism is already free in our stack.

Per-step is a smell — it means the main model is acting blindly and the classifier is compensating for weak steering. The right granularity is **sparse, at high-leverage points only**.

Point-checking → **region-bounding**. The agent operates freely inside the envelope and escalates at the border. The clean observer is ideal for boundary-definition — no step-by-step tunnel vision.

The biased model is structurally altitude-blind: deep in the flow it has momentum + tunnel vision, so the dangerous act "looks routine." The unbiased observer, at plan time, has altitude + no momentum, so it pre-flags the specific high-stakes moments. Clean division: **executor + boundary-definer**.

Flips the observer from **advised** to **enforced**. Two-tier: (1) ACL/envelope (pre-computed by observer at plan time) — covered call → auto-allow; uncovered → route to tier 2. (2) Observer blast-radius check — safe → allow; high-blast-radius → escalate to human. Slots into opencode's existing permission layer.

User noted opencode v2 ACLs have native scope. I verified the v2 permission module (`packages/opencode/src/permission/`): the Rule is `{ permission, pattern, action }` (`packages/schema/src/v1/permission.ts`), per-agent (`agents.&lt;name&gt;.permissions`), ordered. The envelope is natively expressible as the per-agent `permissions` array.

Verified: `tool.execute.before` exists in **v1** (`packages/plugin/src/index.ts`): `(input: { tool, sessionID, callID }, output: { args: any }) =&gt; Promise&lt;void&gt;`. It's an Effect: allow = return, modify = mutate `output.args`, deny = throw, async → can spawn a subagent. The whole design is a **simple v1 plugin**.

User asked to check the webfetch-guard plugin docs. Read `~/.config/opencode/plugins/02-opencode-webfetch-guard.js` + `webfetch-guard.jsonc`: config is a `permission.webfetch` glob → action map; most-specific glob wins (specificity = literal non-wildcard char count); no match = allow (fail-open). Two hooks: `tool.definition` (appends guard note) + `tool.execute.before` (the gate). On deny, rewrites URL to a local sentinel (guard server on 127.0.0.1). The `ask` case currently passes through + logs.

The hook is **blocking** (awaited before `item.execute`), so it *is* the "ask" mechanism. No gap; the observer call is just the content of the ask-branch inside the hook.

Wrote the design as a theme file `docs/research/blast-radius-observer.md` (164 lines): key insight (clean-context subagent = unbiased observer), per-step is a smell → region-bounding, implementation via v1 `tool.execute.before` (blocking: allow/modify/deny/async→subagent), the webfetch-guard precedent (config map, most-specific-glob-wins, gate, sentinel), full design (generalize to per-tool + `ask` → observer), v2 native scope (alternative), honest limits (calibration, shared capability blind spot, envelope maintenance). Added README index row + finding #42. Commits d1bd88a, 33a6d6c.

