## 9349-9377 · 2026-10-14 · Closed the webfetch-guard OSS label-order deny gap
The user reported that a webfetch to a hallucinated routify-file-proxy OSS URL (`routify-file-proxy-sg.ap-southeast-1.oss.aliyuncs.com/...?OSSAccessKeyId=...&amp;Signature=...`) was not blocked by the webfetch-guard plugin. I refused to assume the rule exists and works, and read the actual plugin source and config. The guard's `tool.execute.before` gate was firing (the log showed it blocking the same hallucination class at 06:12:00), but the deny glob `*.oss-*.aliyuncs.com` requires a literal `.oss-` (oss **hyphen**); the user's host has `oss` as its own dot-separated label (`...1.oss.aliyuncs.com`), so there is no `.oss-` substring, no rule matches, and the plugin **fails open to allow**. I proved this against the plugin's own `globToRegex` matcher, not by eyeballing. Two secondary findings: the plugin's `DEFAULT_WEBFETCH = {}` is pure allow-all (lines 82-85), and its doc comment at line 53 falsely claims the built-in default is `{ "*.aliyuncs.com": "deny" }` — harmless while the config file exists, silent-disappearance if it is ever deleted.

U: 2. only if it will also be attached to aliyuncs.com part because there's no guarantee that this kind of argument won't happen anywhere else
So I made the fingerprint rule `*aliyuncs.com*OSSAccessKeyId=*Signature=*` (requires the aliyuncs host **and** the fake-signed args in the same URL), and added `*.oss.aliyuncs.com`. Verified 7/7 against the real edited file through the plugin's matcher: both label-order variants deny, `dashscope-intl` allows, a non-aliyuncs S3 host carrying the same `OSSAccessKeyId=`/`Signature=` args allows (exactly the user's condition), github allows. I also corrected my own earlier caution: a **config-only** edit does **not** cache-bust — the augmented tool description (`GUARD_NOTE`, lines 67-73) is a static string that never embeds the pattern list — so no prefill cost. The config is loaded once at plugin init (`loadConfig` line 192, `cfg` captured), so the running server needs an opencode **restart** to pick up the new rules; I did not restart (standing "don't force a prefill").

The user then asked whether the fingerprint rule subsumes the host rules. I proved the three cover three distinct regions: OSS host with no args (host rules only), aliyuncs non-oss host with args (fingerprint only), OSS host with args (overlap) — all non-redundant. The user proposed collapsing to `oss*.aliyuncs.com`; I proved that as literally written it is **anchored at the start** (`globToRegex` → `^oss.*\.aliyuncs\.com$`), so it matches only hosts *beginning* with "oss" (bare regional endpoints) and misses the bucket-first routify hosts — the fix for the idea is a leading star, `*oss*.aliyuncs.com`, which collapses the two host rules into one.

U: well i didn't mean exactly i've asked about pattern, your proposal looks fine, you've already applied it but somehow gone in wrong direction i had to revert
I read disk ground truth: the on-disk file is **already** the correct 2-rule version (`*oss*.aliyuncs.com` + the aliyuncs-scoped fingerprint), parses clean — the user landed it by hand after reverting my 3-rule edit. I did not re-edit.

U: no you've gone looking into cpp code which is unrelated to this session just validate
I validated the real on-disk file: 7/7 pass (deny: `.1.oss.` URL, `.oss-` variant, bare regional endpoint, aliyuncs+fake-args; allow: dashscope-intl, non-aliyuncs host with same args, github). Honest scope: this is a matcher-level test (config + the plugin's `globToRegex` copied verbatim, run on the real file), not a live wire test; the running server holds the old cfg until restart, so the end-to-end block can only be confirmed after that restart.

