## 9389-9436 · 2026-10-14 · Diagnosed and stripped the shared-stealth-browser MCP residual from Paseo agent records
The user opened by asking whether the shared-stealth-browser MCP was enabled in opencode config. I traced the failing turns to the MCP entry itself, not the config: the entry was baked into two Paseo agent records (`7259a0a9`, `ad44d19d`) at browser-attach time (Sep 30) with no lifecycle tie to the plugin, so the plugin's version-gating death (paseo-plugin.json compat `^0.9.0 || ^0.10.0`, dead under 0.11.2) never removed it. The Paseo provider (`opencode-agent.ts:5257`, `startTurn → ensureMcpServersConfigured → mcp.add`) reads the agent *record*, not the plugin registry, so the 0.11.2 gate is invisible to this code path. A direct handshake proved the server binary initializes in seconds — so the hang is in the plugin-injected spawn: the MCP server's first act is connecting to the supervisor socket `psb-2479e8c30a5cf335.sock`, which is stale (its listener was the orphan I'd killed), and the supervisor can never respawn because the plugin is dead, so the server waits forever → opencode's `mcp.add` hits its 30s timeout → `turn_failed`. And it retries every turn because the provider nulls the cached setup promise on failure (`opencode-agent.ts:5266-5267`). So: a stale attach-time injection outliving its plugin, with no teardown — a real lifecycle flaw in the fork.

U: plugin is dead right now since it's version gated, why the hell does this happens?

I laid out three fixes (A: strip the `mcpServers` entry from the two agent JSONs; B: bump compat to include `^0.11.0` + re-enable + stale-socket cleanup; C: the proper fork lifecycle fix). The user chose A first. I stripped only the `shared-stealth-browser` key from both records (preserving serialization style, `.pre-mcpstrip.bak` backups) and recorded B (which also fixes ratex-formula the same way) and C in smart note #25.

The user restarted Paseo and the entry was back. I diagnosed the resurrection: the provider reads the **live session's in-memory config** (`this.config.mcpServers`); my 11:17 disk strip was overwritten because the old daemon kept the entry in memory, the app quit flushed the dirty copy back to disk, the new daemon at 11:26 loaded the re-polluted record, the turn failed, and turn-end flushed it once more (file mtime 11:27:30 = the flush itself). Disk edits can never win while a live session holds the dirty config — the daemon is the writer, flushing on quit and on turn-end.

U: opencode stores session data in sqlite for a while now mind you

Good catch — so I checked opencode's own SQLite for a persisted stealth entry (targeted, no LIKE-scan of the 6.8 GB of blobs): clean, opencode.db holds no MCP registrations. That left the daemon's memory-flush as the only hazard, fixable only in the window where the daemon is dead. I gave a one-liner (guard `pgrep -x "Paseo Daemon"`, then strip `config.mcpServers` from **all** `~/.paseo/agents/*/*.json` with backups) and, when asked, a paired revert script (same daemon-dead guard, restores every `.pre-mcpstrip.bak`). The user ran the strip: verified clean — new daemon booted 11:33:47, all records `mcpServers={}`, no fresh timeouts. Pollution arc closed; B and C remain pending in note #25. Leftovers confirmed inert for the B-fix: stale Sept-30 socket, `.runtime-51a9abff` dir + `ticket-bindings.json` + `profiles` (avito cookies, keep), `.tmp/paseo-shared-stealth-browser` fork copy + `web-stealth-browser.py`.

## 9437-9446 · Verified opencode v1 has no per-agent context size
The user asked whether opencode allows setting max context size per agent. I checked the actual installed v1.18.30 agent schema (`packages/opencode/src/agent/agent.ts`) in the bundled JS rather than recalling: the agent `Info` schema has name, description, mode, native, hidden, topP, temperature, color, permission, model{providerID,modelID}, variant, prompt, options, steps — **no limit field**. Context size is strictly model-level: `models.&lt;id&gt;.limit.context` in `opencode.jsonc`, consumed by compaction/overflow via `overflow.ts → usable()`. My first answer got swallowed by tool-call interleaving; the user replied "you didn't respond" and I repeated it cleanly.
Workaround that works: alias the model entry with a smaller `limit.context` and bind it via `agent.model` (e.g. a `qwen3.8-flash-next-64k` alias of the same endpoint) — same weights/KV, only the client-side budget (compaction trigger, overflow check) changes. Caveats: MC's model-context-limits registry is keyed by model id and self-learns from `context_length_exceeded`, so a new alias id gets learned once naturally; MC's per-agent model override currently covers historian/dreamer only, not session agents. Recorded as memory #1567 (ARCHITECTURE).

## 9447-9475 · 2026-10-17 · Removed the 4x tool_output truncation override
The user recalled that after earlier wrong troubleshooting we raised the toolcall output truncation window, and asked me to verify from config "for certain". I read the global config locally first (`~/.config/opencode/opencode.jsonc:10`): `"tool_output": { "max_bytes": 200000, "max_lines": 8000 }` — a 4× raise over v1 defaults (2,000 lines / 50 KB). The code-consumption half — that v1.18.30's `tool-output-store.ts` `limits()`/`bound()` actually consume those keys — I verified from the v1.18.30 git tag on GitHub. Semantics: output ≤8000 lines AND ≤200 KB passes to the model whole; over either limit the full output is written to `~/.local/share/opencode/tool-output/tool_&lt;id&gt;` (7-day retention, hourly sweeper) and the model sees a head/tail preview (~half the limits each side) with a `... output truncated; full content saved to &lt;path&gt; ...` marker. The limits are per tool call, not per turn; turn-level totals are governed by MC compaction. Evidence the machinery is live: that store directory already holds spills from today's over-limit outputs.
U: why do you gone to github? it should be in our local config, not across the earth
I explained the split: the config answer was 100% local (read first); the code-consumption answer went remote because the local install is a compiled Bun binary (Homebrew `Cellar/opencode/1.18.30_2/bin/opencode`, 147 MB single-file bundle, compressed JS payload, no readable `.js` beside it). I proposed a standing fix: a one-time sparse checkout of `v1.18.30` into `~/Git/opencode` (packages/core, packages/schema, packages/opencode only) so future source questions don't cost a GitHub round-trip. The user didn't command it, so the proposal stands.
U: this change was applied because of different error surface and now bites our butt when toolcall has massive output filling half of a context window in a second, let's remove this override
I backed up the file, removed the `tool_output` block from global `opencode.jsonc`, validated it parses, and confirmed no workspace/project config shadows it (workspace has no `tool_output` block, so defaults apply). From the next server restart, one huge tool call occupies at most ~50 KB of context; the rest goes to the spill file with the path handed in the preview. Memory #1676 (CONFIG_VALUES) recorded the removal.

## 9476-9498 · 2026-10-18 · Diagnosed Paseo chat-outline rail perf and matched upstream fix
The user asked whether the left scroll rail in Paseo can be changed via a plugin — their sessions are massive and they suspected it degrades performance by rendering every "line". I investigated the Paseo source (`~/Git/paseo`, task-explored): the rail is `ChatOutlineRail` (`packages/app/src/agent-stream/chat-outline/rail.web.tsx`, web-rendered, active in Electron). It is far cheaper than it looks: ticks are one per **user prompt** (server-built index, `timeline-prompt-index.ts`), memoized Views in fixed 8px flex slots, no client text measurement. The real per-scroll cost near it is `reportReadingPosition` (`strategy-web.tsx:720-746`, `querySelectorAll` + `getBoundingClientRect` per scroll frame). Plugin answer: **no** — the plugin surface (`docs/plugins.md`) contributes providers, lifecycle hooks, buttons, timeline items, slash commands, composer attachments, own-settings panels, themes; there is no DOM/CSS injection point and no override of built-in chrome. A built-in toggle exists: Settings → Chat → chat outline (`chat-section.tsx`, key `chatOutlineEnabled`, default true). I suggested an A/B: toggle it off and compare scroll smoothness.
U: it's not reduces performance during scrolling, but during rendering model turns, disabling it does seems to help, good catch
The user's A/B corrected the symptom surface, and it matched the code exactly: the cost is the **hook, not the ticks** — `useChatOutline` runs a `latestPromptSeq` reduce over the **full loaded items array** (`use-chat-outline.ts:70-76`) on **every stream update** (many/sec during a model turn), so cost = O(loaded items) × stream events/sec; the tick Views stay memo-inert. Memory #1714 (ARCHITECTURE). I offered to draft an upstream issue.
The user said to check upstream first. Searched getpaseo/paseo: **#4816** (incl. the outline recompute) was fixed by **PR #7052** (merged 10-15) — tail contribution memoized per epoch, only the head scanned per stream delta. v0.11.4 (the user's app) is unfixed (the O(items) reduce sits verbatim); v0.12.0-beta.1 is fixed. Recommendation: leave the rail off on 0.11.4 as the complete mitigation; when next on a beta, run rail-on on a massive session — smooth turns is the packaged-desktop confirmation #4816's close-out lacked, worth a comment on #4816, not a new issue.
Closing question about the compressed rail on beta: the 8px compression is cosmetic and permanent (#7052 didn't touch layout); the fix targeted the data recomputation. Residual costs on beta: the scroll listener (untouched by #7052), ~33 ms p95 per streamed text update (maintainers' own measurement), and N prompt ticks laid out once at timeline load.

## 9499-9545 · 2026-10-18 · Established hosted-Zen subagent routing (model-id format + background gate)
The user opened by asking whether I can spawn a subagent on a different model than the session's current one. My first answer was too narrow — I read the `provider.ninfer.models` block in `opencode.jsonc`, found one entry, and concluded the spawn menu is one model.
U: 2. that's weird because in UI it shows a bunch of free opencode models as well
That was a source-of-truth correction: the daemon's model list is authoritative, and it merges opencode's Zen hosted catalog independently of my provider config. I asked the daemon directly and got 11 models: 10 free hosted Zen (longcat-2.5-preview, space-bunny, mimo-v2.6-flash, nemotron-3-ultra, nemotron-3.5-lightning, nemotron-3.5-super-vl, glm-5.3-flash, deepseek-v4.1-flash, beam, big-pickle) + local `ninfer/qwen3.8-27b-nvfp4`.

In the same exchange I called the local model "27B", and the user corrected my vocabulary:
U: "local 27B for decisions" it's only a label we actually planned to change, in fact you are 3.8-flash-next
The label `qwen3.8-27b-nvfp4` is the intentionally-pinned config string (renaming costs ~9 refs across 4 live files + 62 Paseo records); what's served behind it rotates — today's tenant is qwen3.8-flash-next. I'd been reading capability off the label string, the exact defect class pinned in project rules; I wrote memory #1723 recording label vs served reality.

Then the user framed the routing policy:
U: it's more like, if model is free and i have internet exploration task i don't really need to use local model for that, they also can be useful for unbiased validation, for example
Cleaner than my framing and it carries a privacy boundary: internet exploration is public-data work, so hosted-free costs nothing private, and unbiased validation is strictly better from a *different* model family in fresh context at zero local-KV cost. Pinned as policy #1724.

The user then flagged that `background:true` doesn't work out of the box. I first said the first background spawn is permission-gated; the user rejected that framing:
U: no it needs to be exactly enabled somewhere, otherwise it instantly errors-out
I searched the opencode source and found the gate: `task.ts:99` — `runInBackground &amp;&amp; !flags.experimentalBackgroundSubagents` throws "Background subagents require OPENCODE_EXPERIMENTAL_BACKGROUND_SUBAGENTS=true". The enable is an env var on the opencode server process; since the Paseo daemon spawns the opencode servers, the var must be in the daemon's launch env (plus `~/.zshrc` for terminal runs). Same block: `subagent_depth` default 1 (nesting beyond one level errors — relevant because Paseo agents *are* opencode sessions spawning subagents); `task` calls also hit a normal ask-permission on `subagent_type` (first spawn of a new type prompts).

To prove the Paseo path, the user told me:
U: retry paseo path, but this time read an error
The first probe's spawn succeeded and then opencode itself rejected the model id: "Model not found: opencode/zen:big-pickle. Did you mean: big-pickle?" — my `zen:` namespace was an invention from the model *labels*; the daemon catalog ids are plain. Retried with the exact catalog string `opencode/big-pickle`: spawned clean (agent ad63974d, background forced true for agent-scoped callers per `paseo-tools.ts:1455-1456`, the `background` key stripped from my schema) and the probe returned `PROBE_OK`. Chain proven end-to-end: spawn → hosted free-model turn → clean reply → async finish notification. Archived both probes, wrote smart note #41 with the open questions (tool-call quality on free Zen models, rate limits under fan-out, latency from this network — to be measured on real delegations, not assumed).

