## 9653-9701 · 2026-10-24 · Installed superpowers plugin to fix zero skill auto-fire
U: "btw skills we installed doesn't seems to advertise themselves in proper manner, i didn't seen model using those superpowers skills for documenting"

Measured the census from the DB: since Oct 21, doc-writing skills fired zero times (technical-writing 2 = both manual, writing-for-agents 1 = manual, writing-skills 0). Coding skills (cuda-skill 4, paseo-mermaid 5, go-* 4) fire naturally because their trigger vocabulary matches what the task says. Root cause: description-competition against task framing with no guaranteed channel — selection is pure LLM over the description list, no embeddings, no ranking.

U: "obra superpowers actually seems to have their own plugins for each and every harness"

Pulled their repo: 12 harness adapters (.claude-plugin, .opencode, .pi, etc.), each wiring their bootstrap injection. The mechanism: `experimental.chat.messages.transform` hook re-attaches a ~700-token bootstrap (the full `using-superpowers` SKILL.md — "MUST invoke a relevant skill before any response") at the head of the first user message, on every provider call. Never persisted, never system prompt, fixed prefix position → cache-stable. Three options costed: A = AGENTS.md anchor (~25 tok, repo-only), B = their plugin (~700 + ~500 registry descriptions, global), C = our own thin plugin (~100 tok, global, no duplication).

U: "check that B cost isn't actually a misinterpretation since it was made by 3.8-27B in conditions when we built our own prompt ruleset for it specifically"

Audited the inherited "meta-derailer" label against the doc: `skills-install.md:104-108` tags it [secondhand], reasoning is analogy to DCP's measured failure, conditioned on the 27B tenant + pre-rebuild ruleset. The derailer was never measured — the README's deferred item literally says "A/B the derailing against." The agent committed the "recall verdicts by scope" defect: reused by the shared word ("derailer") instead of its measured scope. Corrected: B is not ruled out; the derailer risk is an open hypothesis.

U: "'duplicate registry entries' isn't it only because we installed their skills directly? considering that they never fire up we can replace them with proper plugin, like there's no point having them and doing nothing anyways"

Confirmed: the duplication is self-inflicted (we copied systematic-debugging and verification-before-completion out of their repo in September); census shows zero calls for both. The bootstrap is a general mandate ("invoke relevant skills") — it advertises every skill in the registry including our 18, not just theirs. Decision: install the plugin, remove the orphan dirs + writing-for-agents (zero calls).

U: "hmm, you've said that it injected with each message, now you claim that it's injected with first message, can you please just properly check how plugin works, it doesn't seems to be big"

Read `prompt.ts` v1.18.30 + `superpowers.js` from source. The loop (`:1090-1092`) reloads `msgs` from DB on every iteration (one per provider call in a tool loop). Right before request build (`:1255`): `plugin.trigger("experimental.chat.messages.transform", ...)` fires on that fresh array. The plugin (`superpowers.js:244-268`): finds first user message, `unshift`s bootstrap at its head, marker guard keeps exactly one copy, child sessions skipped. One copy re-attached per step, zero in storage.

Asked why they don't inject system prompt like MC does. Answer: MC doesn't use system either — same channel class (user-message content). Two recorded reasons: **#894** "multiple system messages break compatibility with qwen3.5 models" (our served tenant is a Qwen finetune), **#750** token bloat on system channel (sits outside cached prefix, worse per-provider cache behavior). Plus 12-harness portability. Superpowers bootstrap is static → fixed early position → caches for free; MC's history mutates → deliberate cache-bust positions.

PI adapter analyzed: same doctrine — synthetic `role:"user"` message at head, `context` event (fires before each LLM call), lifecycle flags `session_start` → inject on, `agent_end` → off, `session_compact` → on again. Suggests PI injects into transcript (persists) vs opencode's wire-copy path; [inferred] from flag design.

AGENTS.md compatibility audit: initially found "one direct contradiction" (their commit steps vs NEVER-tier approval rule). 

U: "'NEVER treat agreement, engagement, or discussion of a topic as approval for an action' it's not a commit discipline, it's gate to prevent model to rush for implementing before discussion actually concluded plan"

Corrected: the rule gates discussion→execution boundary, not per-action approval during execution of an approved plan. Their chain satisfies it (brainstorm → plan → explicit review gate → execution). Contradiction withdrawn. Residual: two tensions (bootstrap "before any response" vs terseness; task-reviewer subagents vs "you keep validation") — behavioral, to be settled by A/B. Final: zero direct contradictions.

U: "ok, we can remove their skills then, remove writing-for-agents since it doesn't works anyway and install their plugin then"

Began install: checked their INSTALL doc, confirmed v7.0.1 (published yesterday), started config edit + skill removal. Chunk ends mid-install (rm hit deny rule, retry with `rm -r` pending approval).

