## 9760-9784 · 2026-10-26→27 · Investigated Permission.disabled mechanism for hiding task tool
User asked whether opencode's native `task` subagent tool can be disabled so only Paseo subagents are visible. I traced the mechanism in v1.18.30 source: `Permission.disabled()` (`packages/opencode/src/permission/index.ts:204-219`) marks any tool whose last matching rule is `{pattern:"*", action:"deny"}`, and `visibleTools()`/`resolveTools()` (`session/llm/request.ts:208`) filter those out of the request entirely — the tool never appears in the model's schema list. A bare `"deny"` in config compiles to exactly that rule.

U: "is it possible to disable opencode subagent tool, so only paseo subagents would be visible?"

I proposed the one-line config addition (`"task": "deny"` in the global permission block) and enumerated consequences: (1) global — every session loses native subagents including my explore/coder dispatch path; (2) MC unaffected (historian/dreamer make their own harness calls); (3) superpowers chain loses its legs — their V1 tool mapping routes `Subagent (general-purpose):` → `task`, so `subagent-driven-development` and `dispatching-parallel-agents` would dispatch into a nonexistent tool; (4) the two-systems confusion it solves is real.

U: "paseo subagent isn't general purpose subagent?"

I clarified: functionally yes — Paseo agents are full general-purpose agents, strictly more capable for delegation. Four real deltas: spawn path (in-process vs daemon RPC), visibility (invisible vs UI-visible), permissions (parent's set vs own flow), prompt vocabulary (superpowers' mapping text says `task`). The deny is cleaner than initially framed — the chain's dispatch capability exists under Paseo, just re-plumbed.

U: "so if we add deny on tool it will dissapear from announcement?"

Confirmed: removal fires only when the last matching rule is blanket `pattern:"*" → deny`; a partial ruleset leaves the tool announced but failing on those calls. Precedent: same mechanism removed `question`/`plan_exit` from the wire earlier.

U: "so how would change look like?"

I showed the exact one-line addition to the existing global permission block and noted the per-project escape hatch (`"task": "allow"` in project opencode.json).

