## 9802-9819 · 2026-10-29 · Issue 52400 recall and Paseo bridge direction clarification
User asked to be reminded what issue 52400 was about. I retrieved from ctx_search (nothing in this project's archive) then checked upstream trackers directly: it was our filing on `anomalyco/opencode` — the plugin log-noise defect. With any plugin loaded (even a 3-line no-op), the v1 server logs `schema rejection kind=Payload reason="Expected object, got undefined"` once per turn. An internal POST to its own HttpApi sends a body missing an object field; the 400 is swallowed. The offender is unidentifiable from logs because `schema-error.ts` has `context.endpoint.path` in scope but doesn't log it. Disposition: closed by thdxr's October v1-triage sweep ("OpenCode 2.0 is out, closing issues filed against v1"). Not fixed, not wontfixed — swept.

U: "from what i remember it's paseo injecting something, what?"

I retrieved the bridge audit (from ordinals 5196-5219): the bridge is `~/.paseo/runtime/opencode/paseo-*.mjs` (547 KB, mostly inlined zod), wired via a 279-char `OPENCODE_CONFIG_CONTENT` env. It does two things: (1) at load, fetches a tool manifest from the daemon and registers each entry as a `paseo_&lt;name&gt;` tool; (2) per turn, a `shell.env` hook. No system-prompt injection. But the causal link to #52400: we explicitly ruled the bridge out — the A/B matrix showed the per-turn warn fires with a 3-line no-op plugin, zero tools, zero hooks. Paseo supplied the plugin, not the bug.

U: "well, what i expected that it's a bridge which routes opencode to it's own plugins so paseo plugin could have opencode-side linked inside of it"

I verified against source: it's the opposite direction. The bridge is a tiny localhost HTTP server inside the daemon (`providers/opencode/bridge.ts:151` serves `GET /_internal/opencode/tools`) whose manifest is built from `PaseoToolCatalog` — 3,258 lines of Paseo's own core orchestration tools (create_agent, send_agent_prompt, list_agents, archive_agent, terminals, workspaces, schedules, voice). `paseo-tools.ts` contains zero references to plugins — the plugin system cannot contribute entries to it. So the bridge routes the daemon's built-in agent-management surface into the opencode model as `paseo_*` tools, not a plugin bus. What the user expected exists only as DIY: a plugin calling opencode's own HTTP API directly (exactly the path the shared-stealth-browser fork hand-rolled).

