## 9826-9860 · Diagnosed superpowers plugin's mid-turn injection mechanism and cache-bust-on-restart
The user raised the superpowers plugin's bootstrap re-materialization as a chain-of-thought breaker and asked whether plain skill installation (without the plugin) is a supported path. I checked upstream docs: `.opencode/INSTALL.md:44-57` documents the symlink-based install as deprecated in favor of the plugin, whose two jobs (skill registration + bootstrap injection) are described as inseparable at `docs/README.opencode.md:122`. The user then challenged my "measured fire-rate loss" claim — did upstream ever measure bootstrap superiority over plain install? I searched their test suite and found: no. Their `tests/skill-triggering/prompts/*` suite runs red/green with the bootstrap always active; no arm runs the same scenarios without injection. The `2026-06-10-positive-instruction-redesign-design.md` study measures wording, not placement. The bootstrap's superiority over plain announcement is asserted, never measured.
U: "the measured fire-rate loss" did they measured fire-rate increase after implementing their assumed superior method?
The user then corrected my framing — I had been discussing the compaction cache-bug (#2283 cluster), but the actual symptom is different:
U: i'm not talking about compaction bug, yet it might exist, i'm talking about injection happened, model started to think about instruction, announced action but didn't did it, e.g. those injection in a middle of a model turn broke it's chain of thought
I traced the exact mechanism from the installed source (`superpowers.js:240-263`): the transform fires on every agent step (not just every turn, per their own comment at `:240-241`); the anchor is `output.messages.find(m =&gt; m.info.role === 'user')` — the first user message surviving in the currently loaded window, not the session's first. When MC folds the compartment containing the current anchor, the next step's `find()` lands on a later user message deep in the transcript, and `unshift` drops the ~900-token `EXTREMELY_IMPORTANT` mandate at its head. The dedup guard (`:251`) only checks that specific anchor copy, so it never fires on the new anchor. This is deterministic anchor drift, not random injection.
U: What exactly prevents them from checking whether their message survived or not
I explained three reasons: (1) their design goal is head position, not survival; (2) marker scanning has a demonstrated false-positive class (#2600); (3) they can't reproduce the state (their harness holds the window constant). The user then proposed the correct algorithm:
U: Why would they need to check materialization status if they can just remember where message landed and check current window
I agreed — a process-memory `Map&lt;sessionID, messageID&gt;` with an O(1) membership check is the right instrument, immune to #2600's false-positive class. Their code writes the degenerate case of this algorithm (re-`find()` every step) and it's been indistinguishable from the general one on every substrate they test. The hard part was never the check; it's deciding the anchor-gone policy (their #2283 dilemma).
The user then asked how the plugin persists data to avoid cache bust on restart. I read the installed source and explained: it doesn't persist anything — the injection is a pure function of on-disk state (SKILL.md read once per process + static strings), the `_bootstrapCache` Map is only an optimization, and the block never lands in storage. The version pin (`#v7.0.1`) is the persistence guarantee. The user tested this claim directly:
U: i've restarted opencode just to test your claim
U: and it didn't hold, full cache bust
I began investigating the cause — separating the user's observation (full prefill on first post-restart turn) from the mechanism. The established bust causes in this stack are MC's m[0] gate and TTL idle; a restart is an idle gap by construction. I started checking MC's log for which one fired.

